$DIR/main.p:16:2: cannot pass &x to vec.(*Vec).Push, which keeps it after returning
    &x is a pointer to x. x is a local variable of Global (declared at
    main.p:15). vec.(*Vec).Push passes it to rt.InitValue (vec.p:152), which
    stores x in *p (str.p:522), so &x would outlive x.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If vec.(*Vec).Push should keep its own copy of x, change it to take the
    value, not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[int] from new, a
    shared[int], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:24:2: cannot pass &y to vec.(*Vec).Push, which keeps it after returning
    &y is a pointer to y. y is a local variable of Local (declared at
    main.p:21). vec.(*Vec).Push passes it to rt.InitValue (vec.p:152), which
    stores x in *p (str.p:522), so &y would outlive y.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If vec.(*Vec).Push should keep its own copy of y, change it to take the
    value, not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[int] from new, a
    shared[int], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:39:2: cannot store &z in b.p[0], which outlives z
    &z is a pointer to z. z is a local variable of Through (declared at
    main.p:38). When Through returns, z's memory is reclaimed, but b.p[0] may be
    on the heap and live longer, so it would be left pointing at reclaimed
    memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value instead of a pointer to it, changing the type of b.p[0] to
    match. This copies z, or moves it if it is an owner:
        b.p[0] = z
    Or allocate z and store its owner, an owned[int], which moves into b.p[0]:
        b.p[0] = new(int)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
